ML_PAGE("l7-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l7\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 7 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain, apply to the scenario, and justify. The model answers below show the standard to aim for; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l7\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>Four ways to learn: separation, probability, similarity, transformation</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>SVM</b><p>Where should the boundary go? The widest margin, decided by support vectors.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>Naive Bayes</b><p>Which class is most probable given the evidence? Prior x likelihoods.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>KNN</b><p>What happened to the most similar past cases? Vote of the K nearest.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>Neural network</b><p>Which transformation of the inputs fits? Weighted sums, activations, backpropagation.</p></div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Naive Bayes is used for spam.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Names a use case but explains nothing.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Naive Bayes estimates the posterior probability of each class from the class prior and the likelihood of each observed word, assuming the words are conditionally independent given the class. Counting priors and word likelihoods is fast even with thousands of sparse word features, which makes it a strong spam baseline, although the independence assumption is unrealistic, its probabilities may be poorly calibrated, and unseen words need Laplace smoothing.\"</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain the role of support vectors and the maximum margin in SVM. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l7-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l7-1\">An SVM separates classes with a hyperplane $w^Tx+b=0$ - a line in 2D, a plane in 3D. Among all separating hyperplanes it chooses the one with the largest margin, the distance to the nearest observations that constrain it, because a wide margin is more tolerant of small variations in new data. Those constraining observations are the support vectors: they alone determine the position and orientation of the boundary, so moving any other point leaves it unchanged. In soft-margin SVM, the parameter C controls how strongly violations are penalized: larger C narrows the margin to fit the training data, smaller C widens it.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain the kernel trick and why it is useful. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l7-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l7-2\">Some classes cannot be separated by a straight line in the original features, such as a disc of points surrounded by a ring. The kernel trick lets an SVM compute similarities that correspond to a higher-dimensional feature space - for the rings, adding $x^2+y^2$ makes a flat plane separate them - without explicitly constructing every transformed feature. Kernels such as polynomial and RBF therefore give flexible nonlinear boundaries. The kernel and its parameters, and C, must be tuned, and features should be scaled because the similarities are distance-based.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain how Naive Bayes classifies, its independence assumption and one limitation. (8 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l7-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l7-3\">Naive Bayes applies Bayes' theorem, $P(C\\mid X)=P(X\\mid C)P(C)/P(X)$: the prior $P(C)$ is updated by the likelihood of the evidence to give the posterior. Assuming the features are conditionally independent given the class, the likelihood becomes a product, so each class is scored as $P(C)\\prod_iP(x_i\\mid C)$ and the highest score wins; $P(X)$ is common to all classes. In the lecture's example, Score(Yes) ≈ 0.00370 and Score(No) ≈ 0.00703, so Buy = No. It is fast and effective for text, but the independence assumption is often unrealistic and a feature value never seen in a class gives zero likelihood, which Laplace smoothing fixes.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EVALUATE</span>\n        <h4>Explain how the choice of K affects KNN performance. (6 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l7-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l7-4\">KNN stores the training data and predicts a new observation from its K nearest neighbours, usually by Euclidean distance: majority vote for classification, the average for regression. A small K gives very local decisions; with K = 1 a single noisy or mislabelled neighbour decides, which overfits. A large K smooths the decision but includes less similar, distant observations and can underfit by ignoring local structure. K should therefore be chosen by validation (odd values avoid ties in binary problems), and the features must be scaled so the distances are meaningful.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain why nonlinear activation functions are necessary and how XOR illustrates it. (8 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l7-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l7-5\">Each neuron computes a weighted sum $z=\\sum w_ix_i+b$ and passes it through an activation function, for example sigmoid, tanh or ReLU. Without a nonlinear activation, stacked layers collapse into a single linear transformation ($3(2X)=6X$), so depth adds no expressive power. XOR outputs 1 when exactly one input is 1; its positive cases lie on opposite corners of the input square, so no single straight line separates the classes. A hidden layer of nonlinear neurons draws two boundaries whose combination isolates those corners - with the lecture's weights, input (0, 1) gives $H_1\\approx0.9241$, $H_2\\approx0.00022$ and an output of about 0.94, class 1.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain how an ANN learns and discuss the risk of overfitting. (10 marks)</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l7-6\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l7-6\">A neural network consists of input, hidden and output layers of neurons that compute weighted sums and nonlinear activations. Training starts from small random weights (not all zero, which would leave neurons identical - the symmetry problem). Each epoch performs a forward pass to produce predictions, a loss function such as mean squared error or cross-entropy measures the error against the known targets, backpropagation uses the chain rule to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, and an optimizer updates each weight with $w_{new}=w_{old}-\\eta\\,\\partial L/\\partial w$, where the learning rate must be neither too large (divergence) nor too small (slow training). Because networks are highly flexible, they can memorize the training data; early stopping, dropout and more representative data help control overfitting.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l7\" id=\"l7s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">11 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"LECTURE 7 - SVM, Naïve Bayes, KNN and Artificial Neural Networks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s0\" title=\"LECTURE 7 - SVM, Naïve Bayes, KNN and Artificial Neural Networks\">Lecture 7 overview</button></li><li data-full=\"Part A - Support Vector Machine\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s1\" title=\"Part A - Support Vector Machine\">SVM</button></li><li data-full=\"Part B - Naïve Bayes\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s2\" title=\"Part B - Naïve Bayes\">Naive Bayes</button></li><li data-full=\"Part C - K-Nearest Neighbours\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s3\" title=\"Part C - K-Nearest Neighbours\">KNN</button></li><li data-full=\"Part D - Artificial Neural Networks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s4\" title=\"Part D - Artificial Neural Networks\">Neural networks</button></li><li data-full=\"Part E - XOR: The Most Important Neural-Network Illustration\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s5\" title=\"Part E - XOR: The Most Important Neural-Network Illustration\">XOR illustration</button></li><li data-full=\"Part F - How Neural Networks Learn\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s6\" title=\"Part F - How Neural Networks Learn\">How networks learn</button></li><li data-full=\"Part G - Choosing and Comparing Algorithms\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s7\" title=\"Part G - Choosing and Comparing Algorithms\">Choosing algorithms</button></li><li data-full=\"Part H - Exam Answer-Writing Framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s8\" title=\"Part H - Exam Answer-Writing Framework\">Answer-writing framework</button></li><li data-full=\"Part I - Practice for the Subjective Exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s9\" title=\"Part I - Practice for the Subjective Exam\">Practice question bank</button></li><li data-full=\"Final - Reconstruct the Whole Chapter From Memory\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s10\" title=\"Final - Reconstruct the Whole Chapter From Memory\">Reconstruct the chapter</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l7\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Lecture 7 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>11 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">LECTURE 7 - SVM, Naïve Bayes, KNN and Artificial Neural Networks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Lecture 6 introduced algorithms that learn equations or decision rules.</p>\n<p>Lecture 7 presents four other ways to approach supervised learning:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Algorithm</th>\n<th>Fundamental learning idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Support Vector Machine (SVM)</td>\n<td>Find a decision boundary that separates classes effectively</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naïve Bayes</td>\n<td>Use probability and observed evidence to determine the most likely class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN</td>\n<td>Predict using the most similar historical observations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Artificial Neural Network (ANN)</td>\n<td>Learn complex transformations through interconnected weighted neurons</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Think of these as four different approaches to the same problem.</p>\n<p>Suppose you're classifying whether a banking transaction is fraudulent.</p>\n<p>SVM asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Where should I draw the boundary between fraudulent and legitimate transactions?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Naïve Bayes asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Given this transaction's characteristics, which class is more probable?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>KNN asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What happened to the most similar previous transactions?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>ANN asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What complex relationship between the inputs and fraud outcomes can I learn?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Understanding those distinctions is the foundation of Lecture 7.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part A - Support Vector Machine</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l7_svm\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Maximum margin and the C parameter</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · A real soft-margin SVM. Drag points and change C to see the margin, the support vectors and the violations.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>1. SVM: finding the best separating boundary</h4>\n<p>Imagine plotting bank customers using two features:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1=Income$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_2=Debt$$</div>\n<p>Customers are labelled:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red  → Default\nBlue → No Default\n</code></pre>\n<p>Suppose the two groups can be separated by a straight line.</p>\n<p>Many different lines might separate them.</p>\n<p>The question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which line should we choose?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>SVM seeks a separating hyperplane with the largest possible margin, under the model's constraints.</p>\n<p>This is the principle illustrated on slides 51-60.</p>\n<h4>2. What is a hyperplane?</h4>\n<p>A hyperplane is a decision boundary.</p>\n<p>Its linear equation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{w^TX+b=0}$$</div>\n<p>Depending on dimensionality:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Number of input dimensions</th>\n<th>Decision boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Plane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher dimensions</td>\n<td>Hyperplane</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>SVM uses the features as coordinates in a mathematical space and attempts to separate the classes.</p>\n<h4>3. Why maximize the margin?</h4>\n<p>Imagine two possible decision boundaries.</p>\n<p>Boundary A passes extremely close to observations from both classes.</p>\n<p>Boundary B leaves a larger gap between the boundary and the nearest observations.</p>\n<p>If new observations vary slightly, Boundary A may be more susceptible to changing its classification.</p>\n<p>Boundary B potentially has greater tolerance to such changes.</p>\n<p>That gap is the margin.</p>\n<p>The observations that constrain the margin are called:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Support Vectors}}$$</div>\n<p>These are the training points that directly influence the final separating boundary.</p>\n<p>The maximum-margin and support-vector relationships are illustrated on slide 56.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain the role of support vectors and maximum margin in SVM. (6 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Support Vector Machine is a supervised-learning algorithm that identifies a decision boundary, called a hyperplane, for separating observations into classes. Its objective is to obtain a separating boundary with a large margin, which is the distance from the boundary to the nearest observations that constrain it. These influential observations are known as support vectors. Maximizing the margin can improve the robustness of classification to small variations in new observations. Therefore, support vectors are important because they determine the position and orientation of the decision boundary.</p>\n<h4>4. Soft-margin SVM: what happens when classes overlap?</h4>\n<p>Real-world data rarely separates perfectly.</p>\n<p>Consider loan defaults.</p>\n<p>Two customers may have nearly identical income and debt levels but different repayment outcomes.</p>\n<p>A classifier demanding perfect separation might become excessively sensitive to unusual observations.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces the parameter:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{C}$$</div>\n<p>in soft-margin SVM.</p>\n<h5>What does C control?</h5>\n<p>C determines how strongly margin violations are penalized during optimization.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Larger C</th>\n<th>Smaller C</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Greater penalty for violations</td>\n<td>Weaker penalty for violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tries harder to fit training classifications</td>\n<td>Allows more violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May produce narrower margins</td>\n<td>May permit wider margins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher risk of fitting noise</td>\n<td>Stronger regularization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is the relationship presented on slides 57-58.</p>\n<h5>Important distinction</h5>\n<p>A larger C does not mean a larger margin.</p>\n<p>It generally means the model places greater emphasis on penalizing violations.</p>\n<p>You should also distinguish the lecture's hard-margin illustrations from soft-margin SVM: soft-margin optimization can accept classification errors when that yields a better trade-off under its objective.</p>\n<h4>5. Why do we need kernels?</h4>\n<p>Now imagine that the observations form concentric circles.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Inner circle → Class A\nOuter ring   → Class B\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can one straight line separate the inner circle from the outer ring?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>That's because the classes are not linearly separable in the original input space.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces nonlinear SVM and the kernel trick on slides 63-66.</p>\n<h5>Kernel intuition</h5>\n<p>Instead of forcing a linear boundary in the original feature space, a kernel allows the algorithm to work with similarities corresponding to another, potentially higher-dimensional feature representation.</p>\n<p>Different kernels represent different forms of similarity.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kernel</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear</td>\n<td>Approximately linearly separable data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polynomial</td>\n<td>Polynomial relationships</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RBF</td>\n<td>Flexible nonlinear boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel is an important example in the lecture.</p>\n<h5>Why is scaling relevant?</h5>\n<p>Because SVM's geometry and similarity calculations can be affected by the numerical ranges of its features.</p>\n<p>If Income ranges from RM2,000 to RM200,000 while Age ranges from 20 to 70, the numerical units may distort the model's calculations.</p>\n<p>Therefore, scaling is generally important.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain the kernel trick and why it is useful for nonlinear classification. (6 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>The kernel trick allows SVM to model nonlinear relationships by computing similarity functions corresponding to an alternative feature space. In the original feature space, some classes may not be separable by a straight line, such as observations arranged in concentric circles. A suitable nonlinear kernel, such as the RBF kernel, allows SVM to construct more flexible decision boundaries without explicitly generating every transformed feature. Consequently, kernel SVM can handle certain nonlinear classification problems that a linear SVM cannot represent adequately. However, the choice of kernel and its parameters must be tuned appropriately to avoid poor generalization.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part B - Naïve Bayes</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l7_bayes\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Naive Bayes step by step</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · The lecture&#x27;s ten customers. Change the new customer, compare class scores, and fix zero frequencies with Laplace smoothing.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>6. Naïve Bayes: reasoning from evidence</h4>\n<p>Suppose a bank wants to identify suspicious transactions.</p>\n<p>Before examining a particular transaction, it knows that only a small percentage of historical transactions are fraudulent.</p>\n<p>That gives the bank an initial belief about the probability of fraud.</p>\n<p>Now a new transaction arrives:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Unusually large amount\nUnfamiliar location\nUnusual transaction time\n</code></pre>\n<p>This new information may change the estimated probability of fraud.</p>\n<p>Naïve Bayes formalizes this process using Bayes' Theorem.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces this idea on slides 69-73.</p>\n<h4>7. Understand the components of Bayes' Theorem</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nP(C\\mid X)=\\frac{P(X\\mid C)P(C)}{P(X)}\n}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$P(C)$</td>\n<td>Prior probability of class C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$P(X\\mid C)$</td>\n<td>Likelihood of seeing evidence X given class C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$P(C\\mid X)$</td>\n<td>Posterior probability after observing X</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$P(X)$</td>\n<td>Probability of the observed evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The central logic is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nPrior\n+\nEvidence\n\\rightarrow\nUpdated\\ Belief\n}$$</div>\n<h5>Prior</h5>\n<p>Before knowing anything about this specific customer, what proportion of customers historically default?</p>\n<h5>Likelihood</h5>\n<p>If a customer belongs to the Default class, how likely are the observed characteristics?</p>\n<h5>Posterior</h5>\n<p>After observing those characteristics, what is the probability that the customer belongs to the Default class?</p>\n<p>This is the distinction you must explain in a subjective exam.</p>\n<h4>8. Why is it called Naïve?</h4>\n<p>Naïve Bayes makes a simplifying assumption:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The features are conditionally independent given the class.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Mathematically:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nP(X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\mid C)=\n\\prod_{i=1}^{n}P(X_i\\mid C)\n}$$</div>\n<p>Instead of calculating a complex joint probability for every possible feature combination, the model multiplies individual feature likelihoods.</p>\n<h5>Intuitive example</h5>\n<p>Suppose you're predicting whether someone will buy an umbrella using:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Rain\nClouds\nHumidity\n</code></pre>\n<p>These features are obviously related in real life.</p>\n<p>But Naïve Bayes simplifies the calculation by treating them as conditionally independent after the target class is specified.</p>\n<p>That's why it is called naïve.</p>\n<h5>Why can it still perform well?</h5>\n<p>The assumption may not be perfectly realistic, but the resulting probability estimates may still rank the competing classes sufficiently well for useful classification.</p>\n<p>It is computationally efficient and is commonly used as a baseline for text classification, spam detection and document classification.</p>\n<h4>9. Worked Naïve Bayes example</h4>\n<p>Your lecture uses Customer Buying Behaviour on slides 74-76.</p>\n<p>The target is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Buy\\in\\{Yes,No\\}$$</div>\n<p>The input features are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Weather\nTemperature\nHumidity\nWind\nDay\nPromotion\n</code></pre>\n<p>The new customer is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Sunny\nCool\nHigh Humidity\nWeak Wind\nWeekend\nPromotion = Yes\n</code></pre>\n<p>The goal is to predict whether the customer will buy.</p>\n<h5>Step 1 - Prior probabilities</h5>\n<p>There are 10 historical customers.</p>\n<p>Six purchased, and four did not.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Yes)=\\frac6{10}=0.6$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(No)=\\frac4{10}=0.4$$</div>\n<p>These are the priors.</p>\n<h5>Step 2 - Likelihoods</h5>\n<p>For every feature value in the new instance, estimate its frequency within each class.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Sunny\\mid Yes)=\\frac16$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Cool\\mid Yes)=\\frac36$$</div>\n<p>The Naïve Bayes assumption lets us multiply these individual probabilities.</p>\n<h5>Step 3 - Compare class scores</h5>\n<p>For classification, it is sufficient to compare:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(C)=P(C)\\prod_iP(X_i\\mid C)$$</div>\n<p>because the evidence denominator $P(X)$ is shared across the candidate classes.</p>\n<p>Slide calculation note: The frequency counts and arithmetic reported on slides 75-76 do not fully agree with the 10 source rows shown on slide 74. Using those displayed rows directly, without smoothing, gives the following values:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(Yes)=\n\\frac6{10}\n\\left(\n\\frac16\\cdot\\frac36\\cdot\\frac26\\cdot\n\\frac46\\cdot\\frac36\\cdot\\frac46\n\\right)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Score(Yes)\\approx0.00370}$$</div>\n<p>For No:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(No)=\n\\frac4{10}\n\\left(\n\\frac34\\cdot\\frac14\\cdot\\frac34\\cdot\n\\frac24\\cdot\\frac24\\cdot\\frac24\n\\right)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Score(No)\\approx0.00703}$$</div>\n<p>Since:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(No)&gt;Score(Yes)$$</div>\n<p>the predicted class is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Buy=No}$$</div>\n<p>This agrees with the slide's final classification, although the corrected intermediate value for Yes differs from the slide.</p>\n<h5>Step 4 - Posterior probabilities</h5>\n<p>Normalize the two scores:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(No\\mid X)=\n\\frac{0.00703}{0.00370+0.00703}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P(No\\mid X)\\approx0.655}$$</div>\n<p>Similarly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P(Yes\\mid X)\\approx0.345}$$</div>\n<p>The values should be understood as estimates under the model's independence assumption and the frequency estimates used.</p>\n<h5>What the examiner wants</h5>\n<p>You should be able to explain the calculation in this order:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nCalculate\\ Priors\n\\rightarrow\nCalculate\\ Likelihoods\n\\rightarrow\nMultiply\n\\rightarrow\nCompare\\ Scores\n}$$</div>\n<p>If actual posterior probabilities are requested, normalize the scores.</p>\n<h4>10. The zero-frequency problem</h4>\n<p>Suppose a feature value never appeared among the training examples belonging to one class.</p>\n<p>Then its estimated likelihood may be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(X_i\\mid C)=0$$</div>\n<p>But Naïve Bayes multiplies probabilities.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0\\times Anything=0$$</div>\n<p>The entire class score becomes zero.</p>\n<p>This is the zero-frequency problem.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces Laplace smoothing on slide 73.</p>\n<p>For categorical features, add-one smoothing can be expressed as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nP(X_i=v\\mid C)=\n\\frac{Count(v,C)+1}{Count(C)+K}\n}$$</div>\n<p>where $K$ is the number of possible categories for that feature.</p>\n<p>The purpose is to prevent unseen feature values from automatically producing zero likelihood estimates.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain how Naïve Bayes works, its independence assumption and one limitation. (8 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Naïve Bayes is a probabilistic supervised classification algorithm based on Bayes' Theorem. It estimates the posterior probability of each target class using the class prior and the likelihood of the observed features. To simplify these calculations, it assumes that the input features are conditionally independent given the class. This makes the method computationally efficient, especially for tasks such as spam detection and document classification. However, the conditional-independence assumption may be unrealistic when features are strongly correlated. Another limitation is the zero-frequency problem, where an unseen feature value produces a likelihood of zero and may eliminate a class from consideration. Laplace smoothing can be used to reduce this problem.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part C - K-Nearest Neighbours</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l7_knn_k\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Choosing K in KNN</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · KNN decision regions for small and large K, with leave-one-out accuracy - and what happens when the features are not scaled.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>11. KNN: why learn a complex equation when you can compare examples?</h4>\n<p>Imagine a new student with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Study Hours = 5\nAttendance = 80%\n</code></pre>\n<p>You want to predict whether the student will pass.</p>\n<p>Instead of estimating a global equation, you find the most similar historical students.</p>\n<p>Suppose the three nearest examples are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Student A → Pass\nStudent B → Pass\nStudent C → Fail\n</code></pre>\n<p>Using majority voting:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Prediction=Pass}$$</div>\n<p>That's the basic idea of K-Nearest Neighbours.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes this algorithm on slides 79-87.</p>\n<h4>12. Why is KNN called lazy learning?</h4>\n<p>Most supervised algorithms train by estimating parameters or constructing a model.</p>\n<p>KNN generally stores the training examples and performs much of the computational work when a new observation arrives.</p>\n<p>During prediction, it must identify the nearest historical observations.</p>\n<p>This is why the lecture calls it a lazy learning algorithm.</p>\n<p><strong>Consequences</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Advantage</th>\n<th>Limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Relatively simple training process</td>\n<td>Prediction can be computationally expensive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intuitive similarity-based logic</td>\n<td>Must retain training examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can represent nonlinear relationships</td>\n<td>Sensitive to feature scaling and dimensionality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>KNN is also described as non-parametric because it does not assume one fixed finite-dimensional parametric relationship between inputs and targets.</p>\n<h4>13. How does KNN work mathematically?</h4>\n<p>The standard steps are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose the number of neighbours $K$.</li>\n<li>Calculate the distance between the new observation and training observations.</li>\n<li>Identify the $K$ nearest examples.</li>\n<li>Use majority voting for classification or aggregate numerical outcomes for regression.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecture uses Euclidean distance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nd(A,B)=\\sqrt{\\sum_{j=1}^{n}(A_j-B_j)^2}\n}$$</div>\n<p>For two observations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A=(2,3)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$B=(5,7)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d=\\sqrt{(5-2)^2+(7-3)^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{9+16}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{d=5}$$</div>\n<p>This distance gives the algorithm a measure of similarity.</p>\n<p>Smaller distances indicate closer observations under the chosen metric.</p>\n<h4>14. Why is choosing K important?</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$K=1$$</div>\n<p>The model looks only at the nearest observation.</p>\n<p>That can make it highly sensitive to noise.</p>\n<p>If the nearest historical example has an incorrect label, the prediction may also be incorrect.</p>\n<p>Now consider:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$K=100$$</div>\n<p>The model uses many more examples.</p>\n<p>This produces a smoother decision, but it may include observations from areas that are not very similar to the new case.</p>\n<p><strong>K and model complexity</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Small K</th>\n<th>Large K</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Highly localized decisions</td>\n<td>More generalized decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher sensitivity to individual examples</td>\n<td>Less sensitivity to individual examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greater risk of overfitting</td>\n<td>Greater risk of underfitting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture discusses these ideas through its KNN algorithm and neighbourhood examples on slides 81-87.</p>\n<h5>Another important issue: scaling</h5>\n<p>KNN relies on numerical distance.</p>\n<p>If Income ranges from RM2,000 to RM200,000 while Age ranges from 20 to 70, Income may dominate distance calculations merely due to its units.</p>\n<p>This connects directly back to Lecture 5.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain how the choice of K affects KNN performance. (6 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>KNN predicts outcomes using the most similar training observations, where K specifies the number of neighbours considered. For classification, the most common class among the neighbours determines the predicted label. A small K produces highly local decisions and may be sensitive to noise or mislabeled observations, increasing overfitting risk. A large K produces smoother predictions but may include less relevant observations and overlook local patterns, increasing underfitting risk. Therefore, K should be selected using validation performance rather than choosing the smallest or largest value automatically.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part D - Artificial Neural Networks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>15. ANN: learning through interconnected weighted neurons</h4>\n<p>The previous algorithms are relatively easy to explain.</p>\n<p>Neural networks can represent far more complex relationships, but the building block is surprisingly simple.</p>\n<p>An artificial neuron receives inputs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$$</div>\n<p>Each input has a weight:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_n$$</div>\n<p>The neuron calculates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=\\sum_iw_iX_i+b}$$</div>\n<p>Then applies an activation function:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{a=f(z)}$$</div>\n<p>Your lecture introduces this artificial-neuron structure on slide 92.</p>\n<h5>Intuition</h5>\n<p>Think of the weights as adjustable parameters that determine how strongly inputs contribute to the neuron's calculation.</p>\n<p>The bias shifts the weighted sum.</p>\n<p>The activation function transforms the result before passing it to the next layer.</p>\n<h4>16. Understanding a neuron calculation</h4>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1=3,\\quad X_2=4$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_1=2,\\quad w_2=-1,\\quad b=1$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=2(3)+(-1)(4)+1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=6-4+1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=3}$$</div>\n<p>Now apply sigmoid:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$a=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-3}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{a\\approx0.953}$$</div>\n<p>This is a basic forward-pass calculation.</p>\n<p>A neural network performs many such calculations across its layers.</p>\n<h4>17. Why do neural networks need hidden layers?</h4>\n<p>A network typically contains:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Input layer</td>\n<td>Receives original features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hidden layer</td>\n<td>Learns intermediate transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output layer</td>\n<td>Produces the final prediction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture illustrates these layers on slides 93 and 97.</p>\n<p>But there is a deeper question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why not simply stack lots of linear equations?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Because stacking only linear or affine operations still produces an affine transformation.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$f_1(X)=2X$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$f_2(f_1(X))=3(2X)=6X$$</div>\n<p>That's still linear.</p>\n<p>You haven't gained a genuinely nonlinear relationship.</p>\n<p>This is why nonlinear activation functions are important.</p>\n<h4>18. Sigmoid, Tanh and ReLU</h4>\n<p>The lecture presents three activation functions on slides 94-95.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n<th>Output</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sigmoid</td>\n<td>$\\frac1{1+e^{-z}}$</td>\n<td>Between 0 and 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tanh</td>\n<td>$\\tanh(z)$</td>\n<td>Between -1 and 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReLU</td>\n<td>$\\max(0,z)$</td>\n<td>Zero or positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5>Sigmoid</h5>\n<p>Useful for transforming an output into a value between 0 and 1, especially for binary classification probabilities.</p>\n<h5>Tanh</h5>\n<p>Produces positive and negative activations and is zero-centered.</p>\n<h5>ReLU</h5>\n<p>For negative inputs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ReLU(-3)=0$$</div>\n<p>For positive inputs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ReLU(5)=5$$</div>\n<p>Its simple form makes it commonly used in neural-network hidden layers.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain why nonlinear activation functions are necessary in deep neural networks. (6 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Activation functions transform the weighted sum computed by a neuron before passing its output to the next layer. Nonlinear activation functions are important because multiple layers containing only linear or affine operations are mathematically equivalent to a single affine transformation. This would restrict the network's ability to represent complex nonlinear relationships. Functions such as Sigmoid, Tanh and ReLU introduce nonlinearity, allowing hidden layers to learn more expressive representations of the input data.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part E - XOR: The Most Important Neural-Network Illustration</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l7_xor\"><div class=\"vis-title\">XOR forward pass</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Step through · The lecture&#x27;s XOR network, one neuron at a time, with the hidden-unit lines drawn in input space.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>19. Why does the lecture spend time on logic gates?</h4>\n<p>The lecture compares AND, OR and XOR on slide 104.</p>\n<p>Their outputs are:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>$X_1$</th>\n<th>$X_2$</th>\n<th>AND</th>\n<th>OR</th>\n<th>XOR</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The key is XOR.</p>\n<p>It outputs 1 when exactly one of its inputs is 1.</p>\n<p>If you plot the four observations, the positive examples occupy opposite corners.</p>\n<p>No single straight line can separate the positive class from both negative examples.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{XOR is not linearly separable}}$$</div>\n<p>This demonstrates why a single linear classifier is insufficient.</p>\n<p>A neural network with an appropriate hidden layer and nonlinear activations can represent XOR.</p>\n<h4>20. Understanding the lecture's forward-pass calculation</h4>\n<p>Slides 105-108 show how an XOR network computes its output.</p>\n<p>The input is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X_1=0,\\quad X_2=1$$</div>\n<p>The first hidden neuron calculates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z_1=2.5$$</div>\n<p>Apply sigmoid:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$H_1=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-2.5}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{H_1\\approx0.9241}$$</div>\n<p>The second hidden neuron calculates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z_2=-8.4$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$H_2=\\frac{1}{1+e^{8.4}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{H_2\\approx0.00022}$$</div>\n<p>The output neuron then combines the two hidden outputs using its own weights and bias:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z_{out}=5.9H_1+5.2H_2-2.7$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z_{out}\\approx2.7533$$</div>\n<p>Apply sigmoid again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat y=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-2.7533}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\hat y\\approx0.9401}$$</div>\n<p>Using a classification threshold of 0.5:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Class=1}$$</div>\n<p>That matches the expected XOR outcome for inputs $(0,1)$.</p>\n<p>The lecture includes another XOR calculation on slide 110 with different weights, producing a prediction near 0.90. Treat those as two distinct numerical examples.</p>\n<h5>What matters for the exam?</h5>\n<p>Understand the sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nInput\n\\rightarrow\nWeighted\\ Sum\n\\rightarrow\nActivation\n\\rightarrow\nNext\\ Layer\n\\rightarrow\nOutput\n}$$</div>\n<p>If given weights, inputs and biases, you should be able to calculate the output one neuron at a time.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part F - How Neural Networks Learn</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l7_lr\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Learning rate</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Animated · Gradient descent on a loss curve. Raise the learning rate from slow, to bouncing, to diverging.</figcaption></figure></div><h4>21. Forward pass, loss and backpropagation</h4>\n<p>So far, we've calculated the output using fixed weights.</p>\n<p>But a neural network initially doesn't know what the best weights are.</p>\n<p>It must learn them from labelled training data.</p>\n<p>The lecture describes the training process on slides 98-100:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nInitialize\n\\rightarrow\nForward\\ Pass\n\\rightarrow\nCalculate\\ Loss\n\\rightarrow\nBackpropagate\n\\rightarrow\nUpdate\\ Weights\n}$$</div>\n<p>Let's understand each step.</p>\n<h5>Step 1 - Initialize weights</h5>\n<p>The network begins with an initial set of weights and biases.</p>\n<h5>Step 2 - Forward pass</h5>\n<p>The input moves through the network, producing a prediction.</p>\n<h5>Step 3 - Calculate loss</h5>\n<p>A loss function measures the difference between predicted and actual outcomes.</p>\n<p>The lecture mentions Mean Squared Error and Cross-Entropy Loss as examples.</p>\n<h5>Step 4 - Backpropagation</h5>\n<p>Backpropagation calculates how the loss changes with respect to the weights throughout the network using the chain rule.</p>\n<h5>Step 5 - Update weights</h5>\n<p>An optimizer uses the gradients to adjust the weights.</p>\n<p>A simplified gradient-descent update is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nw_{new}=w_{old}-\\eta\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}\n}$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\eta=\\text{Learning Rate}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}$$</div>\n<p>is the gradient of the loss with respect to that weight.</p>\n<h4>22. What does the learning rate control?</h4>\n<p>The learning rate determines the size of each optimization step.</p>\n<h5>Too large</h5>\n<p>The optimizer may overshoot good parameter values, oscillate or become unstable.</p>\n<h5>Too small</h5>\n<p>Training may progress very slowly.</p>\n<p>Thus, the learning rate affects optimization behaviour and convergence.</p>\n<p>The lecture discusses this in its neural-network training overview on slide 98.</p>\n<h5>Important weight-initialization clarification</h5>\n<p>An earlier slide, page 91, presents a simplified learning rule beginning with zero weights.</p>\n<p>But slide 96 explicitly corrects this for multilayer neural networks.</p>\n<p>Initializing every hidden-layer weight identically can create a symmetry problem in which neurons learn identical functions.</p>\n<p>The lecture therefore introduces randomized initialization strategies such as Xavier/Glorot and He initialization.</p>\n<p>For subjective questions about modern multilayer neural networks, follow that later clarification.</p>\n<h4>23. Why do neural networks overfit?</h4>\n<p>A sufficiently complex neural network has many adjustable parameters.</p>\n<p>It can learn sophisticated relationships, but it may also become too closely fitted to the training observations.</p>\n<p>The lecture illustrates the training-versus-validation performance problem on slide 101.</p>\n<p>Imagine:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Training accuracy keeps improving.\nValidation accuracy improves initially, then decreases.\n</code></pre>\n<p>This suggests that continued training is no longer improving generalization.</p>\n<p>Two important remedies discussed in the lecture are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early stopping:</strong> Stop training when validation performance no longer improves adequately.</li>\n<li><strong>Dropout:</strong> Randomly deactivate some neuron activations during training to reduce reliance on specific pathways.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Representative additional training data can also help.</p>\n<h5>Likely subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain how an Artificial Neural Network learns and discuss the risk of overfitting. (10 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Model answer:</strong></p>\n<p>An Artificial Neural Network consists of interconnected computational neurons organized into input, hidden and output layers. Each neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs, adds a bias and applies an activation function. Nonlinear activation functions allow the network to represent complex relationships that cannot be captured using a single linear transformation. During training, a forward pass produces predictions, and a loss function measures the difference between those predictions and known target values. Backpropagation calculates gradients of the loss with respect to the network parameters, allowing an optimizer to update the weights. This process is repeated over multiple epochs to improve the model's fit. However, a highly flexible network may overfit the training data, producing poor performance on unseen observations. Techniques such as early stopping, dropout and additional representative training data can help control overfitting.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part G - Choosing and Comparing Algorithms</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>24. The most important comparison in Lectures 6-7</h4>\n<p>A likely subjective question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare different supervised-learning algorithms and recommend suitable approaches for different scenarios.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The strongest answer compares their underlying mechanisms and trade-offs.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Algorithm</th>\n<th>Learning mechanism</th>\n<th>Main advantage</th>\n<th>Main limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear Regression</td>\n<td>Fit linear coefficients</td>\n<td>Simple and interpretable</td>\n<td>Limited to linear form without transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logistic Regression</td>\n<td>Estimate class probabilities</td>\n<td>Clear probabilistic baseline</td>\n<td>Basic linear decision boundary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Tree</td>\n<td>Recursive feature splits</td>\n<td>Interpretable rules</td>\n<td>Instability and overfitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n<td>Ensemble of randomized trees</td>\n<td>Often stronger generalization</td>\n<td>Lower interpretability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVM</td>\n<td>Maximum-margin boundary</td>\n<td>Effective geometric separation</td>\n<td>Scaling and tuning important</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naïve Bayes</td>\n<td>Prior x likelihood</td>\n<td>Efficient probabilistic classification</td>\n<td>Conditional-independence assumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN</td>\n<td>Nearest-neighbour similarity</td>\n<td>Simple and intuitive</td>\n<td>Prediction cost and scale sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANN</td>\n<td>Weighted layers and nonlinear activations</td>\n<td>Flexible nonlinear modelling</td>\n<td>Complex training and overfitting risk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This corresponds to the algorithm-comparison matrix on slide 112.</p>\n<h4>25. Scenario: which algorithm should you recommend?</h4>\n<p>Suppose the examiner gives:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A company has a relatively small labelled dataset containing many numerical features. The classes are approximately separable, but feature ranges differ substantially. Recommend an algorithm and explain the preprocessing required.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You might recommend SVM.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>The scenario emphasizes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Labelled data</li>\n<li>Classification</li>\n<li>Numerical features</li>\n<li>A potentially useful separating boundary</li>\n<li>Different feature scales</li>\n</ul>\n<p>SVM is therefore a reasonable candidate.</p>\n<p>You would also explain the need for scaling.</p>\n<p>But don't conclude that SVM is guaranteed to be best.</p>\n<p>Other algorithms should be evaluated, and the choice of kernel and C should be validated.</p>\n<h4>26. Scenario: transparent bank decisions</h4>\n<p>A bank must predict loan default but wants interpretable results.</p>\n<p>Potential algorithms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Logistic Regression:</strong> Useful when the bank wants a transparent probabilistic baseline and interpretable coefficients.</li>\n<li><strong>A small Decision Tree:</strong> Useful when the bank wants to trace explicit IF-THEN rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Random Forest:</strong> May improve predictions for complex relationships, but individual decisions are less straightforward to explain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A strong answer would compare predictive performance with interpretability requirements instead of declaring one algorithm universally superior.</p>\n<h4>27. Scenario: spam classification</h4>\n<p>An email provider wants to classify messages as Spam or Legitimate using thousands of word-frequency features. Recommend an algorithm.</p>\n<p>Naïve Bayes is a suitable baseline.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>It is computationally efficient and commonly used for high-dimensional text-classification problems.</p>\n<p>However, its independence assumption may be violated, and probability estimates may require further evaluation.</p>\n<p>The appropriate answer isn't simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Naïve Bayes is used for spam.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Explain that its probabilistic likelihood structure and computational efficiency make it suitable.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part H - Exam Answer-Writing Framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>28. How to answer questions requiring comparison or evaluation</h4>\n<p>For most higher-mark questions, use:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nDefine\n\\rightarrow\nMechanism\n\\rightarrow\nApplication\n\\rightarrow\nTradeoff\n\\rightarrow\nConclusion\n}$$</div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare KNN and SVM for classification. (8 marks)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>A strong answer:</strong></p>\n<p>KNN and SVM are both supervised algorithms used for classification, but they operate through different mechanisms. KNN predicts the class of a new observation by identifying its K nearest training examples and applying majority voting. Its strength is intuitive similarity-based prediction, although it can become computationally expensive during inference and is sensitive to feature scaling and irrelevant dimensions. SVM instead learns a separating hyperplane that attempts to maximize the margin between classes, while allowing controlled violations in its soft-margin formulation. Nonlinear kernels extend its ability to represent more complex boundaries. Both methods can be sensitive to numerical feature scales, but SVM requires suitable kernel and regularization tuning, whereas KNN requires selecting an appropriate neighbour count and distance metric. Therefore, their suitability should be evaluated according to the dataset's structure, computational constraints and validation performance.</p>\n<p>Notice that this answer explains how they differ, not just what they are.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part I - Practice for the Subjective Exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>29. Questions I would prioritize</h4>\n<p>Use these to practise writing complete answers without looking back at the explanations.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Explain how OLS fits a regression line and why residuals are squared.</strong> <em>(6 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Residual for each observation: $e_i=y_i-\\hat y_i$ (actual minus predicted).</li>\n<li>OLS chooses the coefficients ($m$, $c$) that minimise $SSE=\\sum(y_i-\\hat y_i)^2$.</li>\n<li>Squaring stops positive and negative residuals cancelling (e.g. $+5$ and $-5$ would sum to 0).</li>\n<li>Squaring also penalises large errors more heavily than small ones.</li>\n<li>Worked example: residuals $+5,-5,+5$ give $SSE=75$.</li>\n<li>A low training SSE does not by itself guarantee good predictions on unseen data.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Compare Linear, Multiple and Polynomial Regression, including overfitting risk.</strong> <em>(8 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Linear: $\\hat y=mX+c$, one predictor, straight-line relationship.</li>\n<li>Multiple: several predictors, $\\hat y=c+m_1X_1+\\cdots+m_nX_n$ - still linear in the coefficients.</li>\n<li>Polynomial: adds powers such as $X^2$ to model curved relationships, while staying linear in the coefficients.</li>\n<li>Higher polynomial degree means more flexibility: degree 1 may underfit a curve, a very high degree may fit noise.</li>\n<li>Overfitting shows as excellent training error but poor validation/test error.</li>\n<li>Choose the degree (complexity) using validation performance, not training error.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Explain how Logistic Regression converts a linear score into a binary class decision.</strong> <em>(6 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compute a linear score $z=w_1X_1+\\cdots+w_nX_n+b$.</li>\n<li>Apply the sigmoid $\\sigma(z)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}$, which maps any $z$ into $(0,1)$ - an estimated class probability.</li>\n<li>$\\sigma(0)=0.5$; large positive $z$ approaches 1, large negative $z$ approaches 0.</li>\n<li>Apply a threshold (commonly 0.5): $P\\geq0.5$ → positive class, otherwise negative class.</li>\n<li>The threshold can be moved when false negatives and false positives have different costs.</li>\n<li>So despite its name it is a classifier, unlike Linear Regression which outputs a continuous quantity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Explain how Decision Trees select split points using Gini impurity.</strong> <em>(8 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gini impurity of a node: $1-\\sum_i p_i^2$; 0 = pure node, 0.5 = maximally mixed binary node.</li>\n<li>For each candidate feature and threshold, split the observations into child nodes.</li>\n<li>Compute the weighted impurity $\\sum_j \\frac{n_j}{n}Gini_j$ of the children.</li>\n<li>Example: children with 10 obs (Gini 0) and 30 obs (Gini 0.4) give $\\frac{10}{40}(0)+\\frac{30}{40}(0.4)=0.30$.</li>\n<li>Choose the split with the lowest weighted impurity (largest impurity reduction).</li>\n<li>Repeat recursively on each child until a stopping rule (depth, minimum samples, purity) is met; leaves predict the majority class.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5. Compare Decision Tree and Random Forest and explain the effect of ensemble learning.</strong> <em>(8 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A single tree gives interpretable IF-THEN rules but can overfit and change a lot with small data changes (high variance).</li>\n<li>Random Forest trains many trees, each on a bootstrap sample (sampling with replacement).</li>\n<li>At each split only a random subset of features is considered, so the trees differ.</li>\n<li>Classification: majority vote; regression: average of the tree predictions.</li>\n<li>Combining diverse, imperfectly correlated trees reduces variance and usually improves generalisation.</li>\n<li>Costs: lower interpretability, more memory and computation; Random Forest reduces overfitting risk but does not eliminate it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>6. Explain maximum margin, support vectors, soft-margin C and nonlinear kernels in SVM.</strong> <em>(10 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>SVM finds a separating hyperplane $w^TX+b=0$ (a line in 2D, a plane in 3D).</li>\n<li>Margin: distance from the boundary to the closest observations; SVM maximises it for robustness to small variations.</li>\n<li>Support vectors: the training points on or inside the margin that determine the boundary's position.</li>\n<li>Soft margin: $C$ sets the penalty for violations - large $C$ fits the training data harder and gives a narrower margin (overfitting risk); small $C$ allows more violations and a wider, more regularised margin.</li>\n<li>Kernels (polynomial, RBF) let SVM build nonlinear boundaries, e.g. for concentric circles, without explicitly computing every transformed feature (kernel trick).</li>\n<li>Because it relies on geometry and distances, features should be scaled; kernel and $C$ should be tuned by validation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>7. Using Bayes' Theorem, explain how Naïve Bayes classifies observations and identify its limitations.</strong> <em>(10 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bayes' Theorem: $P(C\\mid X)=\\frac{P(X\\mid C)P(C)}{P(X)}$ - prior, likelihood, evidence, posterior.</li>\n<li>Naïve assumption: features are conditionally independent given the class, so $P(X\\mid C)=\\prod_i P(X_i\\mid C)$.</li>\n<li>Classify by comparing $Score(C)=P(C)\\prod_iP(X_i\\mid C)$ for each class ($P(X)$ is shared); normalise the scores if probabilities are needed.</li>\n<li>Buying example: $Score(Yes)\\approx0.00370$ and $Score(No)\\approx0.00703$, so predict Buy = No ($P(No\\mid X)\\approx0.655$).</li>\n<li>Limitations: independence is often unrealistic, probabilities may be poorly calibrated, and an unseen feature value gives a zero likelihood.</li>\n<li>The zero-frequency problem is handled with Laplace (add-one) smoothing: $\\frac{Count(v,C)+1}{Count(C)+K}$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>8. Explain KNN and evaluate the effects of selecting small versus large K.</strong> <em>(8 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>KNN is lazy and non-parametric: it stores the training data and does the work at prediction time.</li>\n<li>Compute the distance (e.g. Euclidean $\\sqrt{\\sum(A_j-B_j)^2}$) from the new point to the stored points.</li>\n<li>Take the $K$ nearest: majority vote for classification, average for regression.</li>\n<li>Small $K$ (e.g. 1): very local decisions, sensitive to noise and mislabelled points - overfitting risk.</li>\n<li>Large $K$: smoother decisions but may include dissimilar, distant points - underfitting risk.</li>\n<li>Choose $K$ by validation (odd $K$ avoids ties in binary problems); scale the features, and expect slow prediction on large or high-dimensional data.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>9. Explain the forward pass of an ANN and why nonlinear activation functions are necessary.</strong> <em>(8 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each neuron computes a weighted sum $z=\\sum_i w_iX_i+b$ and then an activation $a=f(z)$.</li>\n<li>Layer by layer, the outputs of one layer become the inputs of the next, until the output layer gives the prediction.</li>\n<li>Example: $X=(3,4)$, $w=(2,-1)$, $b=1$ gives $z=3$ and $\\sigma(3)\\approx0.953$.</li>\n<li>Without nonlinear activations, stacked linear (affine) layers collapse into a single linear transformation.</li>\n<li>Nonlinear activations (sigmoid, tanh, ReLU) let hidden layers learn nonlinear relationships.</li>\n<li>XOR is the classic example: it is not linearly separable, but a network with a nonlinear hidden layer can represent it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>10. Describe ANN training through forward propagation, loss, backpropagation and weight updates.</strong> <em>(10 marks)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Suggested answer points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initialise weights with suitable random values (not all zero, which causes the symmetry problem), e.g. Xavier/He.</li>\n<li>Forward pass: inputs flow through the layers to produce a prediction.</li>\n<li>Loss: compare the prediction with the true target (e.g. MSE or cross-entropy).</li>\n<li>Backpropagation: use the chain rule to compute $\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}$ for every weight, from the output layer backwards.</li>\n<li>Update: $w_{new}=w_{old}-\\eta\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}$; the learning rate $\\eta$ must be neither too large (overshoot or diverge) nor too small (slow).</li>\n<li>Repeat over many epochs; watch the validation error and use early stopping, dropout or more data to control overfitting.</li>\n</ul></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Final - Reconstruct the Whole Chapter From Memory</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>When you're in the exam hall, I want you to be able to mentally recreate these relationships.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Algorithm</th>\n<th>What it fundamentally learns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear Regression</td>\n<td>A numerical relationship through fitted coefficients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logistic Regression</td>\n<td>A probabilistic model for class membership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Tree</td>\n<td>A hierarchy of feature-based decision rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n<td>A combination of diverse tree predictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVM</td>\n<td>A separating boundary with margin-based optimization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naïve Bayes</td>\n<td>Class probabilities from priors and feature likelihoods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN</td>\n<td>Predictions based on local similarity to stored examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANN</td>\n<td>Complex transformations using weighted neurons and activations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>And for the important calculations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Linear:\\ \\hat y=mX+c}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Residual:\\ e=y-\\hat y}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{OLS:\\ SSE=\\sum(y-\\hat y)^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Sigmoid:\\ \\sigma(z)=\\frac1{1+e^{-z}}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Gini:\\ 1-\\sum p_i^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Bayes:\\ P(C\\mid X)=\\frac{P(X\\mid C)P(C)}{P(X)}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{KNN:\\ d(A,B)=\\sqrt{\\sum(A_i-B_i)^2}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Neuron:\\ a=f(\\sum w_iX_i+b)}$$</div>\n<p>For your subjective exam, the most important distinction is this:</p>\n<p>Knowing an algorithm's definition allows you to describe it. Understanding its learning mechanism allows you to explain it. Understanding its assumptions and limitations allows you to evaluate and recommend it.</p>\n<p>That's the level of understanding to aim for across both lectures.</p></div></details></div></details>");
